    "created_at": "2025-07-05T05:32:02Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-07-05T05:32:02Z",
    "body": "> Per spec, it's left as the default: https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-document-origin . Is that not what browsers do?\n\nI tested the behavior of existing engines by running the following on a local server (with the domain `localhost`):\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<script>\n    let policy = window.trustedTypes?.createPolicy('default', {\n        createHTML: (input) => input\n    });\n\n    let unsafeHTML = \"<!DOCTYPE html>\"\n    let maybeSafeHTML = policy?.createHTML(unsafeHTML) ?? unsafeHTML;\n    let newDocument = new DOMParser().parseFromString(maybeSafeHTML, \"text/html\");\n    console.log(`Original document domain: ${document.domain}, new document domain ${newDocument.domain}`);\n</script>\n```\n\nOther engines seem to expect the domains to match, but setting the new Document's origin to the default opaque value causes the new domain to always be empty.\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
